                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Partial on wt/glob-buffer (b6f6449), still Doing. expand.rs's glob buffer is owned: expmeta's cp/enddir into the stack block are a static BString plus a length, growstackto is reserve, stnputs is a set_len-to-the-cursor plus append, and addfnamealt's grabstackstr + STARTSTACKSTR + stnputs re-seed is a copy into the region plus set_len(expdir_len). String-builder sites in expand.rs 16 -> 8, and all eight are over owned buffers -- no growstackto/stnputs/stackblock/grabstackstr from memalloc is left in the file, and the two macro transcriptions the glob buffer needed are deleted. crate::memalloc:: in expand.rs 23 -> 19: zero string builder, two grab seams (grabexpdest, addfnamealt) copying an owned buffer into the region, eight stalloc/ckmalloc for strlist/ifsregion/wcifs and _rmescapes' HEAP arm, nine stack marks. All nineteen are delete-memalloc's. The invariant the buffer is held to, since expmeta recurses one frame per path component: at every point where it can grow, its length is the current frame's expdir_len -- asserted on entry to expmeta, re-established by hand where expdir_len changes mid-frame, and derived from the cursor by the stnputs transcription. Four load-bearing sites written up on plan/decisions/owned-data.md: stnputs takes its length in its cursor (which is how a frame recovers from its own recursion -- appending at Vec::len() emits a/b/c/b/...); addfnamealt's re-seed is the allocator's price, not the algorithm's; the growstackto reservation is exact and an assertion on Vec::capacity() is vacuous because Vec over-allocates too, so both expmeta_rmescapes call sites assert against len; and the top-level call was getting a clean buffer by accident, so expandmeta clears. crates/nsh/tests/glob_buffer.rs is new: three cases, one per property, each confirmed to fail under a mutation of the property it names. Still not converted, all outside this branch's scope: bltin/printf.rs, miscbltin.rs, parser.rs, var.rs, output.rs, input.rs. Fully gated: PASS=61498 FAIL=0 FLAKY=22 XFAIL=1 (sorted_tables in aud_state_var, pre-existing), POSIX 54/657 against the C's 56/655 with the same two differentials, ptydiff 31/0, register 28/28."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T16:24:32.160543578Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Done at af1434d. The node's goal — \"the stack string builder becomes an owned byte vector across parser and expand\" — is met, and it is checkable rather than argued: `crates/nsh/src/expand.rs` and `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs` each hold zero `memalloc::` references, and neither file calls any region-state function (`stalloc`, `stackblock`, `grabstackstr`, `growstackblock`, `growstackto`, `makestrspace`, `stnputs`, `stputs`, the three marks, `stackblocksize`).\n\nIt took four passes rather than one, on three branches plus `wt/strlist`: builder sites 205 -> 91 (parser, nodes, cd, histedit, mystring, exec, jobs, eval, shellmain, mail), then the expansion buffer, then the glob buffer, then `strlist` itself. The reason it could not be one commit is on [dec:nsh:owned-data]: `expdest` was a global cursor into the region that `expmeta` borrowed to encode directory entries, so the buffer could not become an index into an owned `BString` until `memtodest`/`chtodest`/`mbtodest` took their destination as a parameter.\n\nWhat remains is not this node's. Two macros from `memalloc.rs` still expand in `parser.rs` — `USTPUTC` x6 and `STADJUST` x1 — but both are pointer arithmetic (`p += amount`) over a `BString`'s spare capacity and touch no region state; rehoming them is bookkeeping recorded on [[delete-memalloc]]. Builder-shaped code outside parser and expand — `bltin/printf.rs`, `miscbltin.rs`, `var.rs`, `output.rs`, `input.rs` — was never in this node's title and is likewise over owned buffers now."
                            }

