                    {
                        id delete-memalloc
                        title "Move the last region allocations to ownership and delete memalloc.rs"
                        priority 0
                        kind @refactor
                        deps (owned-strings)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T13:46:34.315288286Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Partial on wt/delete-memalloc (ff0de43, 24be8c1, 77fe182), still Todo. memalloc.rs is NOT deleted; the region and the checked-malloc wrappers both survive, with two different owners. Removed: expand.rs's three ckmalloc sites (ifsregion chain -> Vec<ifsregion> with \"empty == ifslastp NULL\" argued across its three writers; wcifs -> Vec<wchar_t>, keeping wcschr's terminator-matching because ifsisifs can reach it with wc==0; _rmescapes' RMESCAPE_HEAP arm -> a caller-supplied buffer, with the stalloc arm shown unreachable because RMESCAPE_ALLOC is only set under FNMATCH_IS_ENABLED, which is 0); output.rs's xvasprintf, whose stalloc(MAX(len, stackblocksize()) + 1) had exactly one customer -- print_escape_str's run of X's -- and that customer had already moved to its own buffer in the previous pass; and the seven region allocations that were copies rather than lists (evalstring's sstrdup/stunalloc pair, evalcommand's argv array, cdcmd's and find_dot_file's and fc -e's padvance candidates, test_access's group array, updatepwd's strtok copy, arith_yylex's variable names). Call sites: stalloc 15 -> 7, stunalloc 1 -> 0, setstackmark 11 -> 8, popstackmark 17 -> 14, stackblocksize 4 -> 2, ckmalloc 16 -> 13, ckfree 33 -> 28. crate::memalloc:: 57 -> 40; bltin/printf.rs 4 -> 0, bltin/test.rs 2 -> 0, histedit.rs 2 -> 0, arith_yylex.rs 1 -> 0, output.rs 5 -> 1 (ckrealloc only), expand.rs 19 -> 15. Not done: struct strlist. All seven remaining stalloc sites are strlist nodes or copies feeding them (grabexpdest, ifsbreakup's fields, addfname_common via sstrdup, addfnamealt), and its readers span expand.rs, eval.rs and miscbltin.rs; it is one commit because strlist.text stopping being a region pointer changes every reader at once, and the marks cannot go before it. Also not done, and out of bounds twice over: ckmalloc/ckrealloc/ckfree/savestr for the hash tables and process-state structs in var.rs, alias.rs, jobs.rs, options.rs, trap.rs, input.rs, redir.rs and exec.rs's tblentry -- another pass owns var.rs/alias.rs and docs/idiomatization.md 2.3 step 4 makes their iteration order a category-3 divergence that cannot land before sanctioned-divergences. Eight load-bearing sites written up on plan/decisions/owned-data.md, the sharpest being that dotcmd's commandname is read by evalbltin's epilogue after dotcmd has returned, so find_dot_file's buffer is moved into a static slot rather than dropped -- and the assertion that a Vec move preserves the pointer is what caught that find_dot_file returns its argument unchanged for a name containing '/'. Also recorded: popstackmark is replaceable by Drop only because raise_longjmp is panic_any under panic=\"unwind\"; that is this port's forced choice (error.rs records that a real longjmp over a catch_unwind-armed jmploc segfaulted), not a property of Rust, and the two paths that still skip destructors -- exraise under vforked, and shellexec reaching execve -- are correct to. All three commits fully gated: PASS=61498 FAIL=0 XFAIL=1 (FLAKY 23/25/20), no STALE; divtest PASS=28 FAIL=0; POSIX 54/657 against the C's 56/655 with the same two differentials; ptydiff 31/0. Every new assertion mutation-checked. No port bug and no C bug found this pass."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T15:50:59.405855145Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`wt/strlist` merged to master at 9d02b1c (four commits, 491d712..741b9e5). The region is now unreachable, not merely unused, and the remaining work is enumerated rather than estimated.\n\nVerified independently of the agent's report, on the merge:\n\nOf the nine macros `memalloc.rs` exports at crate root, seven have zero callers anywhere (`ckfree`, `STARTSTACKSTR`, `STPUTC`, `CHECKSTRSPACE`, `STACKSTRNUL`, `STUNPUTC`, `STTOPC`). Two survive, both in `parser.rs` alone: `USTPUTC` x6 and `STADJUST` x1. Neither touches region state — `STADJUST` is `p += amount` and `parser.rs`'s cursor is a `BString`'s spare capacity, so they are cursor arithmetic that ended up in the allocator's file because `bltin/printf.rs` needed its own copy of `STADJUST` and defines one locally at line 79.\n\n`stalloc` has zero call sites. The one the agent's count attributes to `exec.rs` is `exec.rs:23`'s import; rustc reports it as `unused import: stalloc`, and the only other mentions in that file are two doc comments.\n\nSo what is left to delete the file:\n\n1. Rehome `USTPUTC` and `STADJUST` — seven sites in one file, and `bltin/printf.rs`'s local `STADJUST` is the second copy that says where they belong.\n2. Delete the seven dead macros and `exec.rs:23`'s import.\n3. The checked-malloc wrappers: `ckmalloc`/`ckrealloc`/`ckfree`/`savestr` at 49 sites in `var.rs`, `alias.rs`, `jobs.rs`, `options.rs`, `trap.rs`, `input.rs`, `redir.rs`, `exec.rs`. Another node's data — the hash tables and process-state structs.\n4. The spec-rule half the agent correctly declined: fifteen of `memalloc.rs`'s twenty `def` rules with their `sem` pairs, nine of its thirteen unit tests, and `mystring.rs:sstrdup` whose only caller `addfname` is gone. This moves the `cargo test -p nsh --lib 58` gate number and touches `docs/spec`, which no pass in this node has done, so it is its own commit.\n\nOnly item 3 is work; 1, 2 and 4 are bookkeeping over code nothing reaches.\n\nGates on the merge, all green: build ok (68 warnings, unchanged set); `cargo test -p nsh --lib` 58; streams_embed 4, expansion_buffer 4, glob_buffer 3; divtest PASS=28 FAIL=0; `runall.sh 8` TOTAL PASS=61498 FAIL=0 FLAKY=29 XFAIL=1 with no STALE (the XFAIL is `sorted_tables` in `aud_state_var`); POSIX 657/54 against the C's 655/56 with the same two differentials; ptydiff PASS=31 FAIL=0 FLAKY=0. This sweep is also the first confirmation of `delete-memalloc`'s own merge on master — that node's post-merge sweep died forty corpora in and was never re-run.\n\nCarried forward for [dec:nsh:host-owns-signals], not for this node: removing `popstackmark` removed an `INTOFF`/`INTON` bracket, and the bracket is an interrupt *delivery* point. What is lost is delivery for a SIGINT arriving inside the bracket itself; outside it the handler delivers directly because `suppressint` is zero. It pairs with `docs/errors-are-values.md`'s finding that `sa_flags = 0` leaves every interruptible syscall returning `EINTR`, so a synchronous notice point always exists.\n\nAlso carried forward, for a profile-driven pass: `grabexpdest` is now `mem::take`, so the expansion buffer loses its capacity on every expanded word and regrows from zero. Not visible in sweep wall time, but a real change in allocation behaviour; a swap with a spare buffer would keep both the move and the capacity."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T16:40:02.923371881Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "This node absorbs the container conversions rather than waiting for them, because measuring the remaining `ck*` sites showed they are the same work.\n\nEvery one of the 49 remaining `ckmalloc`/`ckrealloc`/`ckfree`/`savestr` call sites is a container node allocation: `var`/`localvar`/`localvar_list` in var.rs, `parsefile`/`parsefile.buf`/`strpush` in input.rs, `shellparam.p` in options.rs, `procstat` and the `jobtab` realloc in jobs.rs, the `alias` node and its name in alias.rs, `tblentry` in exec.rs (via the C flexible-array trick `size_of::<tblentry>() - ARB + strlen(name) + 1`), `redirtab` in redir.rs, the trap action strings in trap.rs, and the output buffer realloc in output.rs. There is no separate \"remove the allocator\" step left — `memalloc.rs` falls out when the last container becomes an owned Rust value.\n\nTwo things `docs/std-replacements.md` rates as blocked are not blocked any more, and the document predates both. §5.1 rates the hash tables R3 and says they \"cannot land before `sanctioned-divergences`\" — that node is Done, and `sorted-tables` has already run the pattern on two of the three tables. The third is `exec.rs`'s `cmdtable`, and it is observable the same way: `hashcmd` iterates it into `printentry`, and 14 corpus files run `hash`. So it is the same shape of problem with a known-good answer rather than a new risk.\n\nSequenced in three waves, by conflict rather than by size.\n\nWave 1, four agents on disjoint files, launched 2026-08-09 off master 664dab1: `wt/owned-input` (input.rs, 7 sites), `wt/owned-vars` (var.rs, 15), `wt/owned-jobs` (jobs.rs, 5), `wt/owned-small` (redir/trap/alias/options/cd, 16, five separately-gated commits).\n\nWave 2, after wave 1 merges: parser.rs's `synstack` plus rehoming `USTPUTC`/`STADJUST`; exec.rs's `cmdtable` to a `BTreeMap` with a register entry; mystring.rs and system.rs, which are `<string.h>` reimplemented rather than containers.\n\nWave 3: output.rs's `c_vsnprintf`/`VaArg`, which the user chose to pull forward from [[output-is-a-writer]] — it is last because its `out1fmt!`/`outfmt!` macros expand in every file and would collide with every other agent. Then `memalloc.rs` itself and its spec rules.\n\nExplicitly excluded, and the exclusion is the deliverable: `expand.rs`'s `msort`/`pmatch`/`expmeta`, `cd.rs`'s `updatepwd` beyond the `strtok` swap, and the ctype functions. `strcoll`, `fnmatch` and `isalpha` all read the process locale, so replacing them with `Vec::sort`, the `glob` crate or `is_ascii_*` changes glob and sort results. `docs/std-replacements.md` §5.2-§5.5 measures each."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T19:49:09.530072441Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Wave 1 merged: `owned-vars` (f30501e), `owned-small` (8f7bb99), `owned-jobs` (a974bd8), `owned-input` (ba2b8f1). `ck*`/`savestr`/raw-free sites outside `memalloc.rs` go 49 -> 12: var 15->0, input 7->0, jobs 5->0, options 5->0, trap 3->0, redir 2->0. Remaining: expand 4, exec 3, alias 3, output 1, cd 1.\n\nThree C defects found, and the interesting one is the defect that had to be *reproduced*. `popfile` drains only the chain of the level it returns to, so the dying level's `spfree` chain is freed with `ALIASINUSE` still set, and because `pgetc` defers a popped `strpush` by one call a level routinely ends with an entry there. `alias q='echo hi '; echo \\`q\\`; q` makes dash print the diagnostic twice and leaves the alias in use forever. The first `owned-input` commit released the chain -- the obvious thing for an owned `Vec` -- and diverged; a differential probe caught it and the fix became its own commit. This is the single most likely place a later reader \"fixes\" and breaks. Verified on the merge: both shells byte-identical on that probe and on `alias a='alias a=zzz\\necho hi'; a; alias`, and both valgrind-clean.\n\nThe `alias.rs` collision resolved correctly and was the real risk in this wave. Two agents edited the same ownership question from opposite sides: `owned-small` made the node a `Box` and took the address out of the map rather than out of the box, and `owned-input` deleted `setalias`'s `if (!(ap->flag & ALIASINUSE))` guard, because with the pushed text copied there is no second owner to defer to and the guard would only leak. Both changes are in the merged file and the comment states the reason.\n\nTwo more defects in the C, recorded rather than repaired: `popfile`'s `while (pf->strpush)` loop reads the *outer* level's field, so the condition never changes and a second iteration would dereference NULL -- correct in 0.5.10, where `parsefile = pf->prev` came after the loop, and unreachable in the corpus because reaching it segfaults. And `evalpipe` links a job before opening the pipe, so a failed `pipe()` leaves a used zero-process job that `jobs`, `kill %n` and `%string` all read `ps[0]` of; the port answers with the zeroed `ps0` the C reads rather than reading past the end, which is the one place in this wave behaviour was chosen over transcription, and only because the C's is undefined.\n\nCost, measured rather than assumed: `-n` over a 13MB script is 630ms before, 621ms after, C dash 308ms; instruction count +4.3%. Wall clock is inside the noise. The intermediate state -- frames in a `Vec` with `nextc` still a raw pointer -- measured *733ms*, because `pgetc` resolved the frame through an index and a bounds check three times per character; caching the pointer, written only by the single writer of the index and refreshed after each of the two operations that move a frame, closed the whole gap. The index cursor is not what costs.\n\nFollow-ups, none blocking:\n* `docs/std-replacements.md` §4.10 describes an `input.rs` that no longer exists in that form, its line references are stale, and its \"which node\" line points at [[move-state]] rather than here.\n* `jobs.rs`'s `cmdputs` still takes `*const c_char` and `cmdtxt` still passes `b\"...\\0\"` literals -- about 40 mechanical sites. The half that mattered landed: no raw cursor, no `savestr`.\n* `input.rs`'s `release_strpush` keeps a recursive `sp.spfree` walk that is unreachable from its only caller.\n* Two `sem` rules were edited in place -- `input.popfile-fn`, `input.setinputstring-fn` -- because both described dash as it reads rather than as it runs.\n\nProcess, for the next wave and it is a lead's error not an agent's: all four were told to run the full suite at the end and were launched together, so three sweeps collided, load peaked at 88 on 32 cores, swap filled, and two agents ran unscoped `pkill -f runall.sh` that killed each other's runs. Both disclosed it unprompted and neither reported a sweep it believed invalid -- one aggregated 113 per-corpus `dsdiff.sh` tallies by hand instead, with containment untouched. Three of the four sweep results in the wave are void; the one that counts is the single sweep over the merged tree. Stagger the final gates, or have agents stop at cheap gates and let the lead sweep once."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T19:54:26.321052473Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`evalpipe`'s leaked job, carried forward rather than fixed, with the reachability measured rather than assumed.\n\n`eval.rs`'s `evalpipe` calls `makejob(pipelen)` — which links the job into the current-job chain with `used` set — before it opens the pipe, and the \"Pipe call failed\" raise does not free it. What is left behind is a used, zero-process job in a table that `jobs`, `kill %n`, `wait %n` and `%string` all read `ps[0]` of, or `ps[-1]` in the two that index from `nprocs`.\n\nReached on both shells with `ulimit -n 5; echo x | cat | cat`: each prints \"Pipe call failed\" and the outputs are identical. That probe also shows why it is not urgent — `sh_error` is fatal in a non-interactive shell, so the stale entry is unobservable in every `-c` and script case. Observing it needs an interactive shell that survives the diagnostic and then runs `jobs`, which is `tests/harness/ptydiff.py` territory and is not covered by any case there today.\n\nThe port is already the safer of the two: where dash reads past the end of a zero-length process array and hands `outfmt(\"%s\", NULL)` whatever it finds, the port answers with the zeroed `ps0` the C reads. That was the one place in wave 1 where behaviour was chosen over transcription, and only because the C's is undefined.\n\nNot fixed here for two reasons. It is `eval.rs`'s job lifetime rather than `jobs.rs`'s container, so it belongs to whichever pass owns that; and freeing the job would make `jobs` stop listing an entry dash lists, which is a divergence for the register rather than a drive-by. The agent that found it was right not to reach into `eval.rs` for it.\n\nWorth a ptydiff case when `process-model` opens, since that is the only harness that can see it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T20:00:18.580302675Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Wave 1 fully gated on master at f8ee207.\n\n```\nrunall.sh 6   ==== TOTAL PASS=61498 FAIL=0 FLAKY=29 XFAIL=1 ====   no STALE\nptydiff       INTERACTIVE (pty, sandboxed): PASS=37 FAIL=0 FLAKY=0\nPOSIX         cases: PASS=657 FAIL=54    reference: PASS=655 FAIL=56\n              differentials: edit-history-goto-number,\n                             edit-history-search-pattern-anchored\n```\n\nThis is the sweep the whole wave was held for, and it is the only valid one of the five that were started: three agent sweeps were killed by two unscoped `pkill -f runall.sh` calls, and a fourth was invalidated by the disk reaching 100% from a `rustc` build and a `git pack-objects` outside every worktree. It also covers 82dbc38, the `changelocale` fix, which landed after the previous sweep had pinned its binary and so had only cheap gates and valgrind until now.\n\nptydiff is 37 rather than 31 because `owned-jobs` added six interactive cases: five background jobs to push the table past its four slots, `%n`/`%name`/`%?substring`, a two-process pipeline job, `fg`, and ^Z-then-`fg`. That was the right instinct — `corpus-lint.sh` drops signal-delivery cases before they run, so job control is the part of the shell the 61,498-case corpus covers least, and a `Vec<Job>` conversion is exactly the change it would fail to see.\n\nOne loose end recorded rather than chased. The `owned-small` agent's POSIX runs printed a third differential, `locale-jobs-multibyte-command-text: target=PASS/0 reference=PASS/2` — both sides passing, only the reference's exit status differing. It does not appear in either POSIX run on master. `docs/std-replacements.md` §7 already records that `tests/harness/sandboxed.sh` does not pin the locale, so the harness's second configuration axis is whatever the invoking shell happened to export; a differential that appears in one worktree and not another, on a case whose name begins `locale-`, is that gap showing. Pinning `LC_ALL` in the sandbox is a small change to the harness and belongs with [dec:nsh:differential-is-the-oracle], which claims the harness tests exactly one configuration."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T20:51:52.987175342Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
